Z||000000||GTDude||12-16-2001||11:30 AM||||Anyone ever use 1.6 rockers on the 2.8.  Any problems.......what kind of performance increase? Thanks<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||3
Z||000001||Blacktree||12-16-2001||02:06 PM||||I also would like to know. I'm getting ready to rebuild a 2.8 (bored/stroked to 3.2). I planned on using a more aggressive cam and the Comp Cams 1.6 ratio roller tip rockers, among other tweaks. Just wondering what type of performance difference I'd see between the 1.52 ratio and 1.6 ratio rockers.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||12-16-2001||03:39 PM||||I have the Compucam cam and the roller tip rockers. I think you can really notice a difference in the mid-range power and acceleration. The only annoying thing I noticed was that these rockers are a little noisy. One of my club members (NEFA) reported the same thing. He also said that he thinks this is normal. He said he even went as far as taking the engine down to make sure they were adjusted right and they still made noise.I noticed that the latest roller tip rockers are self aligning, this might help with the noise. The noise they make sounds like valves tapping but not as loud. Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||AkursedX||12-16-2001||04:06 PM||||One negative that has not been brought up is that the bigger rockers will give you increased valve guide wear.  <P>But I plan on using these in my rebuild as well.  I think it's a reasonable sacrifice for performance.<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||AnimalGT||12-16-2001||06:22 PM||||Do a search on this folks.  There's a quite alot of info about this on PFF.<BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT GT 87||12-16-2001||08:42 PM||||Comp Cams also has a set of 1.52 roller tip rockers . I'm using GM cam # 12364059 , it has .424 & .454 lift . With the 1.6 I'll be getting close to valve spring bind (about .484 )||||reg||1
Z||000006||GTDude||12-16-2001||08:48 PM||||I'm gonna be using the original stock cam with 135,000 miles on it.  Don't laugh. &lt;smile&gt;<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||3
Z||000007||GT GT 87||12-16-2001||09:24 PM||||GTDude say it is'nt so ! The GM cam I'm using was 108$ with the hi perf lifters !!!!!! Don't go stock ! Unless the engine is not removed . Then with a stock cam , 1.6 would help.||||reg||1
Z||000008||GTDude||12-16-2001||09:50 PM||||Yep, engine is all original with 135k on it.  Runs like a clock.  No noises, no smoke, no oil consumption.  No complaints.  Only mod is a side air scoop and it will turn a 7.92 0-60 which is better than what Road and Track reported in two diff articles for a new 86 GT.  Pretty sure im eventually gonna do the 1.6 rockers and nitrous.  When the engine eventually plays out, im going back with a gen II 3800 supercharged.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||L67Formula||12-17-2001||12:55 AM||||[QUOTE]<BR>[B]Anyone ever use 1.6 rockers on the 2.8.  Any problems.......what kind of performance increase? <P>~5yrs ago I dropped the cradle to have the diff bearings, clutch/pplate/t.o. bearing replaced. While the 2.8 was out I did timing set, compucam/lifters, valve stem seals, high vol. oil pump, and 1.6:1 roller tips.<BR>I didn't want to do a rebuild but with it on an engine stand I couldn't resist doing those items because it was easily accessable.<BR>Would I do it again? Yes. Would I do the rockers as a stand alone mod with the 2.8 in the car? No.<BR>With the cam and rockers it helped the 2.8 perform more like it should have to begin with.<BR>The biggest improvement I got from this project was probably from the timing set. How so? Before the upgrade it used to search for idle speed...it would pulse up and down over a 100-150 rpm range constantly and it really sucked. I think it was from the sloppy timing chain. <BR>Good luck and enjoy your car.<BR>jp<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Blacktree||12-17-2001||02:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><I>I'm using GM cam # 12364059 , it has .424 & .454 lift . With the 1.6 I'll be getting close to valve spring bind (about .484 )</I><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Uh-oh, I didn't even think about valve spring bind. The cam I'm going to use (which I've already bought) has .443 intake & .465 exhaust lift. So mine would be even <I>closer</I> to valve spring bind. Would I be flirting with disaster if I were to use the 1.6 ratio rockers?||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfixit58||12-17-2001||09:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AkursedX:<BR><B>One negative that has not been brought up is that the bigger rockers will give you increased valve guide wear.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, that is a bogus statement. To go from a 1.52 to 1.6:1 the distance from the pivot to the push rod is decreased. The length from the pivot to the valve stem remains the same. If, for some reason, the valve train geometry is off and the rocker arm's tip doesn't ride exactly on through center of the stem (i.e., equal distance from the center of the stem throughout is travel) then, yes, guide wear will increase.<P>What most novice engine builder fail to check when they install a larger camshaft and / or higher ratio rocker arms is valve train geometry and spring bind. To correct spring bind, always use the spring the camshaft maker recommends and then double check it at maximum lift. If bad, the spring seats will have to be fly-cut. The rocker arm geometry can be corrected with custom push rods. Usually, this isn't necessary if rocker arms is your only modification.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <P><B>JUST ADDED: </B>1988 GT w/ T-Tops, 5-Speed, and Performance Sound.||||reg||4
Z||000012||GT GT 87||12-17-2001||06:21 PM||||Valve spring bind depends on the installed height of your valve springs. I'm using 3.4 springs , heads were bought from Shaun . He let me know of that number. It's not only the :bind" , there is a recomened clearance befor this happens. Shaun----Have you any input ????????  Steve||||reg||1
